---
import Article from "../components/article.astro";
import Banner from "../components/banner.astro";
import { Definition } from "../components/definition";
import Heading from "../components/heading.astro";
import Paragraph from "../components/paragraph.astro";
import { LexemeSort } from "../components/suppletion/lexeme-sort";
import { PastTense } from "../components/suppletion/past-tense";
import { WaitUntil } from "../components/wait-until";
import SectionLayout from "../layouts/section.astro";
---

<SectionLayout
  title="Suppletion and lexemes"
  position="5"
  previousPath="/allomorphy"
  nextPath="/conclusion"
>
  <Article>
    <Heading>What’s up with “go” and “went”?</Heading>

    <Paragraph>
      With allomorphy, we saw how an affix can take on different forms when it
      finds itself in different linguistic contexts. However, sometimes the
      addition of an affix completely changes the <b>base</b> of the word:
    </Paragraph>

    <PastTense client:load />

    <WaitUntil client:idle page="suppletion-and-lexemes" progress={1}>
      <Paragraph>
        “Walked” makes perfect sense; it follows the standard rules of
        inflection. “Taught” is a bit quirky, but we can still sort of see the
        historical connection to “teach”.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        But there’s not a single shared letter or sound between “go” and “went”!
      </Paragraph>

      <Definition
        term="suppletion"
        category="noun"
        ipa="səˈpliːʃ(ə)n"
        definition="when an inflected form of a word is completely phonologically unrelated to its base form"
      />

      <Paragraph>
        This morphological phenomenon is known as <b>suppletion</b>. It happens
        when a grammatical change (like changing the tense or making an
        adjective comparative) forces a word into a completely different form.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        We also see this happen with the adjective “good”. Turning it into its
        comparative counterpart changes the form to “better”, which does <i
          >not</i
        >
        share the same base as “good”.
      </Paragraph>

      <Heading>Lexemes</Heading>

      <Paragraph>
        So if “go” and “went” are completely different morphemes, how do we know
        that they’re the “same” word? Or rather <i
          >different grammatical forms</i
        >
        of the same word?
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        To solve this puzzle, linguists rely on the concept of a
        <b>lexeme</b>, which is an abstract unit of meaning that represents a
        complete set of <i>inflected</i> word forms.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        The lexeme <i>go</i> represents the core, abstract concept of moving from
        one place to another. “Go”, “goes”, “going”, “gone”, and “went” are not separate
        words but rather different word <i>forms</i> belonging to the same underlying
        lexeme <i>go</i>.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        We’re able to link completely different phonetic sounds to the same core
        meaning because we store the abstract <b>lexeme</b> in our heads.
        <b>Suppletion</b>, is therefore simply a scenario where a single lexeme
        is realized by word forms that sound nothing alike.
      </Paragraph>

      <LexemeSort client:idle />
    </WaitUntil>

    <WaitUntil client:idle page="suppletion-and-lexemes" progress={2}>
      <Paragraph>
        Exactly! Even though “am” and “was” look like total strangers, they are
        all simply different word forms of the same lexeme.
      </Paragraph>

      <Banner>
        <p>
          You will now see the underlying lexemes for bases in the English
          toolkit, and you’ll also be able to create lexemes to group different
          related morphemes in your conlang!
        </p>
      </Banner>
    </WaitUntil>
  </Article>
</SectionLayout>
